# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the new subsection 5.6c, before 5.7
five_six_c = f"""### 5.6c What v20 changed in the guard and in DB-3, and the specs the v19 review found missing (round 6)

**1. The guard (`evidence-handoff-v20/secdef_guard_v20.py`; its docstring has the same list under "New in v20").** One tokenizer, as before; every item below was found by a reviewer or by the independent runner of v19, shown to work **on the real server (16.15)** in `pg_v20.py` (section "v20" of its output) and shown **missed by the v19 guard** (`secdef_mutants_v20.py`: {mut}).
- **B1 (security blocker).** A `SECURITY DEFINER` function whose body is `RETURN <expression>` was never scanned. v20 scans the expression like any body, and refuses a definer whose head is not `BEGIN ATOMIC` or exactly one string or dollar-quoted body (a definer with nothing to scan is a problem, not a pass). Server proof: a `RETURN query_to_xml(...)` function handed the runtime login a row it cannot read directly.
- **M1.** A call written as `%I.name(` inside an `EXECUTE` string is no longer skipped: the same call rules apply to the string. Server proof: a definer trigger ran a hidden call with the owner's rights.
- **M2.** Triggers are pinned per file (`TRIGGER_PINS_BY_FILE` in `allow_v20.py`: ten in DB-2, four in `0002_audit_chain.sql`). `DROP TRIGGER` of an unpinned name, and any `CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER` whose text differs from the pin (for example `WHEN (false)`), are problems. Control K7 (a v19 check that passed by accident) is rewritten. Server proof: after `DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS` a lowering wrote no record row; a `WHEN (false)` replacement did the same.
- **M3.** `MERGE INTO`, `DELETE ... USING` and `TABLE x` inside an atomic body now count as touching the table they name. The server accepted all three.
- **M4 and F1 (independent runner: 4 of 21 hostile replacement bodies of a listed function were clean).** Each listed definer has a **text fingerprint** (first 16 hex digits of sha256 over its tokens, `FN_PINS_BY_FILE`) and a **statement-kind pin** (`KINDS_BY_FILE`: for example `kill_switch_release` may contain only `UPDATE`; `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`, `MERGE`, `COPY` and a `WITH` that writes are problems). A body that changes behaviour without changing tables (`... WHERE id = 1` instead of `... AND "on" = true`) now changes the fingerprint. **Why text and not a hash of the stored body (`pg_proc.prosrc`):** I did not test that the stored text is the same on 14, 16 and 17 (it should be, but I did not run it), so a catalog hash would be a claim without a run; the start-up query keeps checking the catalog for the properties that do not depend on text (owner, `SET search_path`, grants). **Cost, said plainly:** any edit of a pinned function, even a comment inside the body, needs the pin regenerated (`python -I -B chk.py` prints the new values) and a second independent review.
- **m1 and F2.** Expressions inside the allowed shapes (`CHECK`, `DEFAULT`, `GENERATED ... AS`, index expressions, trigger `WHEN`) are read with the same call rules. Allowed calls are the dated list `CALLS_OK` plus `btrim`, `char_length`, `extract`. 5 of 91 spellings were clean before.
- **m2.** `SET` is allowed after `UPDATE` only in the `SET column = ...` shape, decided by where the statement is, not by a position exemption.
- **m3.** The tokenizer follows the server's own scanner: word characters include all non-ASCII characters, a dollar-quote tag may contain them, and the whitespace set is the server's. Compared with the server on six cases in `pg_v20.py` (m3 lines): the astral-character word and tag cases differed in v19 and agree now. **The form-feed and vertical-tab lines:** the server accepts form feed as a separator and **refuses** vertical tab (SQLSTATE 42601); my tokenizer treats both as separators, which is harmless (a file with a vertical tab cannot be applied anyway).
- **Unit-test sentinel.** `PIN_ANY` switches one pin off in a test; `None` means nothing is pinned, so every listed item is a problem.

**2. DB-3 text changes (held file hash in section 8).** (a) **Agent id (security minor m4):** the NFC and blank-edge rules are replaced by an ASCII allow-list, `CHECK (agent_id ~ '^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._:@/+-]{{0,199}}$')`, on both tables. Reason: `IS NFC NORMALIZED` needs a UTF8 server, and the v19 control-character rule depended on the database's locale. v19 accepted fullwidth letters, a zero-width space, a leading no-break space and a right-to-left override as separate budgets (independent runner, F5); v20 refuses all six with 23514. **This is my own choice of characters, not a lead answer: the lead must confirm that the real agent ids fit (hyphen, dot, colon, at, slash and plus are allowed; the pseudo agents of 5.1 item 5 start with `-` and are never written to this table).** Result on real databases: UTF8 with `C.utf8` and with `C` collation: plain id accepted and the U+0085 id refused in v19 and v20; LATIN1: **v19 refuses every id** (`IS NFC NORMALIZED` fails on a non-UTF8 database, 42601), v20 works; SQL_ASCII: the product's own `migrate()` stops with a `TypeError` before any held file (psycopg returns bytes for names), so a client on SQL_ASCII must be told. (b) **`NOT VALID` is removed** from the agent and day/month checks. `NOT VALID` checks every new version of an old row, so an old row that breaks the rule could never be settled again (shown on a real server in v20, control line m6); now the apply itself refuses (23514) and the whole transaction rolls back (nothing applied: shown). (c) `spend_settle`'s day update now also requires the month row to exist; v19 changed the day row and returned no row when the month row was missing (shown with a control).

**3. Findings of the independent runner of v19 (F4, F5), as facts and tests.** **F5:** `spend_reserve` with amount 0, a negative number, `-Infinity` or `NULL` returns **no row and no error**; `spend_settle` with a negative worst or actual, `-Infinity` or `NULL` also returns no row. **The store code must treat an empty result as an error** (a refusal that is never booked), and the S-2b test list has a row for it (`E0-F5`, below). The day window is enforced at both ends (shown). **F4:** a runtime login can create a `TEMP` table named `spend` and a trigger or rule on it in its own session; the DB-3 functions still write the real table because their `search_path` is `pg_catalog, pg_temp` (temp last); that order is why the `SET search_path` rule of the guard (R2) is a safety rule, not a style rule. Both are tested in `pg_v20.py`.

**4. Start-up check, product inputs (readiness major M4).** (a) **Where the list lives:** `src/hadavin_sdk/store/secdef.py` (new, S-2b), module constants `FUNCTION_POLICY` (name, argument types, owner role, whether it must be a definer, `SET search_path` value, who may execute) and `secdef_problems(connection, policy, roles)`; the prototype is `secdef_audit_v20.py`, which the builder copies, then renames nothing. (b) **Operator role name (L23):** config key `spend.operator_role` (a role name, no default; refused empty when DB-3 is applied). (c) **Owner role:** the role that owns the database (`pg_database.datdba`), read at start-up, never from config. (d) **Behaviour when no DB-2 or DB-3 function exists** (default today): the function checks pass vacuously and the output says so in one line (`no DB-2 or DB-3 function installed: nothing to check`); the L19 check still runs (5.6, L19). (e) It runs on the runtime connection and needs no table privilege (`pg_proc`, `pg_roles`, `aclexplode`).

**5. `limits status --check` (readiness major M3).** Exit **0**: every check passes (or nothing is installed, said in one line). Exit **1**: at least one check fails; one line per check, `PASS name detail` or `FAIL name detail`, no secret and no agent id in a line. Exit **2**: it could not connect or the config is invalid (nothing was checked). Checks: the start-up check of item 4; the kill switch is not on for longer than `spend.kill_switch_alert_minutes` (default 60; my number, no source); the row counts of the two records against the L13 thresholds (1,000,000 rows or 400 days: warning only, exit 0, `WARN` line); the audit reader cannot write (a negative probe in a rolled-back transaction). **Volume (m5):** I measured it on 16.15 (`pg_v20.py`, m5 line): 1000 reserve and settle pairs with actual below the worst case wrote 2000 rows, 270,336 bytes, 135 bytes a row. **So every ordinary settle below its reservation writes two rows once DB-3 is applied: the decrease record is volume, not a tamper signal, and the thresholds above will be reached by volume alone at a few hundred thousand settles.** Default: keep the record as built and filter alerts to the kill-switch records and to decreases not made by `spend_settle`; the lead decides (L24, `v20-OPEN-FOR-LEAD.md`) between that and writing a decrease row only when the new total is lower than the old one (a change to DB-2, which needs a new review round).

**6. DB-3 Grant function kind and the migrate keyword (readiness major M5).** `migrate()` gets one new keyword, `function_grants: Mapping[str, Sequence[str]] | None` (function signature to the roles that may execute it), applied after the files and in the same transaction through the one function `apply_function_grants` (prototype in `secdef_audit_v20.py`: `REVOKE ALL ... FROM PUBLIC`, then `GRANT EXECUTE` to exactly the listed roles, then a re-read of `pg_proc.proacl` that must equal the list, else the transaction fails). The runtime role gets `spend_reserve` and `spend_settle`; the operator role gets `kill_switch_engage` and `kill_switch_release`; nobody else. The store code (`PostgresStore`) calls the functions with `SELECT * FROM fn(...)` and treats an **empty result as a refusal** (item 3). The operator read path (how the operator sees the two records) is `limits status`, which uses the audit-reader login only; nothing else reads them. **What is still open here:** the L-2 store code (5.10b) is dated design only and has no spec beyond that section.

**7. Cutover into DB-3 (security minor m6).** The migration runs inside one transaction (`migrate()` uses `db.transaction()` and an advisory lock; checked in `migrate.py`), so `SET LOCAL lock_timeout` and `SET LOCAL statement_timeout` in the file take effect; **a runner that does not wrap each file in a transaction makes them do nothing (shown: the server only prints a notice)**. Procedure: (1) **drain**: stop the runtime or wait until no reservation is open (`open_usd` is 0 for every row) before applying; (2) before applying, run `SELECT agent_id FROM spend WHERE agent_id !~ '^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._:@/+-]{{0,199}}$'` and the same on `spend_month`; any row it lists must be fixed or removed by the owner, otherwise the apply stops with 23514 (shown on a real server) and **nothing is applied**; (3) apply; the new column `open_usd` starts at 0, so **a reservation made before the cutover is refused at its settle with 23514 and the total stays as booked (the safe direction: spend is never lowered by it)**; with nothing in flight, new reserves and settles work and the total is the old total plus the new amounts (shown); (4) **rollback:** before the commit, the transaction rolls back by itself on any error (shown for the agent-rule case); **after the commit there is no down file** and I did not write one: undoing DB-3 means a new reviewed file that drops the four functions and the two columns. That is stated here rather than built, because a down file for a money table needs its own review.

**8. Hashes (readiness minor 5: v19 abbreviated two different files with similar prefixes).** Full values of the five held files are in section 8; in this section, short forms always carry the file name: DB-2 `spend_decrease_log.sql` `{hs.get('spend_decrease_log.sql', '?')[:16]}...` (unchanged from v19), DB-3 `spend_guarded_writes.sql` `{hs.get('spend_guarded_writes.sql', '?')[:16]}...` (changed in v20).

"""
rep("### 5.7 The guardrail call (paid, metered from S-2a)", five_six_c + "### 5.7 The guardrail call (paid, metered from S-2a)")

